            <ArticleTitle>Reliability and validity of the Faces and Word Descriptor Scales to measure procedural pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>368-74</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Reliability and validity of the Faces and Word Descriptor Scales to measure pain in verbal children undergoing painful procedures were assessed. Test-retest reliability and construct and discriminant validity were supported for both instruments among a sample of 118 children in three age groups (3-7, 8-12, 13-18). Construct validity was determined by comparisons with a visual analogue scale and a numerical scale with known validity. A majority of the children preferred to use the Faces scale when providing self-report of pain regardless of age. The Faces and Word Descriptor Scales are valid and reliable instruments to measure procedural pain intensity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
